From d29e0d213200664326d8e67f7bfa873a35f6978e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mingo <mingo@example.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:21:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(generate): reject unknown grammar keys, warn when an
 extra is shadowed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two ways a grammar author was left to guess.

`grammar.json` was deserialized without `deny_unknown_fields`, so a misspelled
key was dropped on the floor: `"extra"` for `"extras"`, `"precedences"`,
`"inline"` vs `"inlined"`, an unknown key inside a rule node — all generated a
parser from the *remaining* fields and exited 0. A misspelled *field* was caught
only by accident, because the field was then missing. Both the top-level grammar
and the tagged rule enum now deny unknown fields, so the error names the key and
lists the ones that exist.

The lexer report of the previous commit explains why `1 /* c */` lexes `/` as
division, but a report has to be asked for, and an author who does not already
suspect the cause will not ask. A token that completes on the first character of
an extra now warns at generate time, deduplicated per (token, characters, extra)
and capped at ten rows:

  warning: a token that completes at the start of an extra hides that extra:
    `/` completes, so the transition on '*' into an extra is never taken
    at equal length a token beats an extra: no token may be a prefix of an extra
    that must win, so rename the token or change the extra

Token-vs-token shadows stay report-only: they are usually benign. Only separator
shadows warn.
